Your coding agent opens five to ten pull requests a day. Each one is small and
well formed. Each one still pays the same GitHub Actions ritual: full suite,
same minutes, same bill, whether the diff is a comma or a schema migration.

Builders report months where more than half of CI minutes only re-proved work
the branch already ran, then main ran again. Fail-closed gates mean a quota hit
freezes merges while local tests sit green. The agent never saw the invoice.
You did.

**thwip is in public beta** for that loop.

You run the approved checks on the machine that already has the code, the
toolchain, and the agent session. The CLI attaches a signed proof of that exact
Git tree to the commit. A ruleset-protected GitHub App verifies the proof
against current repository permission, enrolled signing keys, and project
policy. The merge queue can require that check. Cloud CI stops being the only
way to make “green” mean something GitHub will enforce.

