## What you install

- **CLI** (`@recallnet/thwip`): runs required checks, signs a compact proof,
  writes a `thwip-Proof` trailer on the commit.
- **GitHub App**: evaluates the exact PR or merge-group subject; owns the
  proof check your ruleset can pin.
- **Control plane** at [thwip.recall.net](https://thwip.recall.net): org and
  project settings mirrored from GitHub. No separate invite list.

Machine setup is pasteable for coding agents: [/install](/install) and
`/install.md`. Repository bootstrap is a separate prompt on each project’s
dashboard page.

## What a proof claims

A proof names who ran the approved checks, on which Git tree, under which
policy version, signed by a key enrolled to a GitHub account that still holds
repository access.

thwip never sees your source and never watches the process. It checks the
signed claim against GitHub facts and a fact bundle from the control plane. A
forged claim has to keep matching every teammate’s digests forever. The first
disagreement is the evidence.

Under **fast**, the signer asserts the local run. Under **remote**, GitHub runs
the required suites and signs for them (SLSA Build L3 for those checks). Set
organization defaults in the dashboard. Override per repository with
`thwip.yaml` on the default branch after protected merge. Candidate PR YAML
never governs its own proof.

{% callout type="tip" title="Pick per path" %}
Use remote where a cloud re-run is worth the minutes: auth, billing, deploy
config, lockfiles. Use fast where you already ran the suite on the tree you
are merging.
{% /callout %}

## Who this beta is for

You ship with Claude Code, Codex, Cursor, or similar. You keep fail-closed
merge gates. You are tired of paying to re-prove the same tree while agents
multiply PRs. You still need something a ruleset can require, and you will not
put the agent inside the trust boundary that decides pass or fail.

If you only want a badge that says “tests passed” without binding signer,
tree, and policy, stop here. The [FAQ](/faq) states what a proof establishes
and what it does not.

## Plans

Free and Pro verify the same way. Free caps projects and signers. Pro raises
those caps. Details stay on the dashboard and in [llms.txt](/llms.txt).

## Start

1. Install the CLI and register a **Signing** key on GitHub (the type defaults
   wrong; Authentication keys do not count).
2. Install the App on your org and enable a repository.
3. In a clone: `thwip init`, then `thwip run` before you push.

